EXT. FLOODED RICE PADDY DIKE - DAWN

Mist on brown water. A narrow dike, the only dry line for a mile. Four soldiers crouch behind a collapsed water buffalo shed.

SERGEANT NIKOLAI BRZEZINSKI (40s, gray stubble, field radio on his back) counts heads. CORPORAL ADAEZE OBI (20s, medic's bag) wraps a bandage around PRIVATE TOMMY "SPARROW" LANGE (19, freckled, pale). PRIVATE HARUTO KAWASAKI (30s, glasses taped at the hinge) watches the treeline through a rifle scope.

The shooting has stopped. Somewhere, a duck complains.

BRZEZINSKI
That's the last of it. Report.

OBI
Sparrow's through-and-through, left arm.

SPARROW
It's fine, it's--

OBI
It's not fine, hold still.

KAWASAKI
Treeline is quiet. Nothing moving.

BRZEZINSKI
Quiet is not nothing.

SPARROW
Sarge, are we gonna wait for the--

OBI
Stop talking.

KAWASAKI
I count two. Maybe three. Walking away.

BRZEZINSKI
Away?

KAWASAKI
Away. Rifles slung.

OBI
(tying off)
They're done too.

SPARROW
How do you know?

BRZEZINSKI
Because they're walking.

A long pause. Brzezinski unslings the radio, thumbs the handset.

BRZEZINSKI (CONT'D)
Tango Two, this is Tango Four. Hold fire. Say again, hold fire.

KAWASAKI
Sergeant, I can see their faces. They're kids.

OBI
So is Sparrow.

SPARROW
I'm twenty in March.

BRZEZINSKI
Nobody is anything in March. Obi, finish. Kawasaki, keep the glass on them till they're gone.

KAWASAKI
Gone.

He lowers the scope. Exhales. Brzezinski sits down hard in the mud.

OBI
Sarge. You're bleeding too.

BRZEZINSKI
Am I?

He looks at his sleeve. Surprised, almost polite.
